from physics_helpers import q,write_bank
items=[
q(1,'What is a vector quantity?','A quantity with magnitude and direction.','4.5.1.1'),
q(1,'Name the force opposing motion through air.','Air resistance.','4.5.1.2'),
q(1,'What is the unit of a moment?','Newton-metre (Nm).','4.5.4'),
q(1,'State the equation for momentum.','p = mv.','4.5.7.1'),
q(1,'What does the gradient of a distance–time graph represent?','Speed.','4.5.6.1.4'),
q(2,'A person walks 30 m east and 10 m west. State the total distance and displacement.','- Distance = 30 + 10 = **40 m**.\n- Displacement = **20 m east**.','4.5.6.1.1','2022 2HR Q03.5: distance/displacement distinction'),
q(2,'Calculate the weight of a 50 kg object where g = 9.8 N/kg.','**W = mg** = 50 × 9.8 = **490 N**.','4.5.1.3'),
q(2,'Forces of 18 N east and 7 N west act on an object. Find the resultant.','18 − 7 = **11 N east**.','4.5.1.4'),
q(2,'A 5 N resultant force acts on a 2.0 kg object. Calculate its acceleration.','**a = F/m** = 5/2.0 = **2.5 m/s²** in the direction of the resultant force.','4.5.6.2.2'),
q(2,'Explain how an object can move at constant speed but have changing velocity.','- Velocity includes **direction** as well as speed.\n- Motion around a circle changes direction, so velocity changes even at constant speed.','4.5.6.1.3'),
q(2,'State the difference between elastic and inelastic deformation.','- Elastic deformation **reverses when the forces are removed**.\n- Inelastic deformation leaves a **permanent shape change**.','4.5.3'),
q(2,'Explain why using a longer spanner can make a nut easier to turn.','- It increases the **perpendicular distance from the pivot**.\n- **M = Fd**, so the same force produces a greater moment.','4.5.4'),
q(2,'Give two factors that can increase thinking distance at the same speed.','- Tiredness can **increase reaction time**.\n- Distraction can **delay the driver’s response**.','4.5.6.3.2','2022 2HR Q03.2–03.3; 2023 2H Q03'),
q(2,'Why does atmospheric pressure decrease with height?','- At higher altitude there is **less air above** a surface.\n- The weight of the air above and its density are lower, so atmospheric pressure is lower.','4.5.5.2','2023 2H Q02'),
q(3,'A spring extends from 12.0 cm to 15.0 cm under a force of 9.0 N. Calculate its spring constant within the proportional region.','- Extension = 15.0 − 12.0 = 3.0 cm = **0.030 m**.\n- **k = F/e** = 9.0/0.030.\n- **300 N/m**.','4.5.3'),
q(3,'A force of 25 N moves a box 2.4 m along the force direction. Calculate the work and describe the main energy transfer if the box moves steadily against friction.','- **W = Fs** = 25 × 2.4 = **60 J**.\n- The force does work against friction.\n- Energy transfers into **thermal stores** of the surfaces and surroundings.','4.5.2','2022 2HR Q01.5–01.6'),
q(3,'A 40 N force acts 0.15 m from a pivot. A second perpendicular force acts 0.30 m on the other side. Find the force needed for rotational balance.','- First moment = 40 × 0.15 = **6.0 Nm**.\n- Balance requires F × 0.30 = 6.0.\n- **F = 20 N**.','4.5.4'),
q(3,'Calculate the pressure due to water at a depth of 3.0 m. Density is 1,000 kg/m³ and g = 9.8 N/kg.','- **p = hρg**.\n- p = 3.0 × 1,000 × 9.8.\n- **29,400 Pa**, above the pressure at the surface.','4.5.5.1.2'),
q(3,'Explain why an immersed object experiences upthrust.','- Liquid pressure **increases with depth**.\n- Pressure on the bottom is greater than on the top.\n- The difference gives an **upward resultant force**.','4.5.5.1.2'),
q(3,'A car accelerates uniformly from 4.0 m/s to 16 m/s in 6.0 s. Calculate its acceleration and state the resultant force if its mass is 1,000 kg.','- a = (v − u)/t = (16 − 4.0)/6.0 = **2.0 m/s²**.\n- **F = ma**.\n- F = 1,000 × 2.0 = **2,000 N**.','4.5.6.1.5–4.5.6.2.2','2022 2HR Q01.2–01.4'),
q(3,'Explain how a falling object reaches terminal velocity.','- Initially weight exceeds drag, so it accelerates downwards.\n- Increasing speed increases **drag**, reducing the resultant force.\n- When **drag equals weight**, acceleration is zero and velocity becomes constant.','4.5.6.1.5','2022 2HR Q04.1–04.2; 2023 2H Q02: force balance'),
q(3,'A book rests on a table. Explain why its weight and the table’s normal force are not a Newton’s third-law pair, and identify the partner to its weight.','- Both named forces act on the **same object**, the book.\n- Third-law partners act on **different objects**.\n- The partner to Earth’s gravitational pull on the book is the **book’s gravitational pull on Earth**.','4.5.6.2.3'),
q(3,'A 1,500 kg car at 20 m/s has a driver reaction time of 0.60 s. Calculate its momentum and thinking distance.','- Momentum = mv = 1,500 × 20 = **30,000 kg m/s**.\n- Thinking distance = speed × reaction time.\n- 20 × 0.60 = **12 m**.','4.5.6.3.2; 4.5.7.1'),
q(3,'Explain how an airbag reduces injury in a collision using momentum.','- The passenger undergoes the same required **momentum change** to come to rest.\n- The airbag increases the **stopping time**.\n- **F = Δp/Δt**, so average force is smaller.','4.5.7.3','2023 2H Q05.3'),
q(4,'An object accelerates uniformly from rest at 3.0 m/s² over 24 m. Calculate its final speed.','- **v² − u² = 2as**.\n- v² = 0 + 2 × 3.0 × 24 = **144**.\n- v = √144.\n- **12 m/s**.','4.5.6.1.5','2022 2HR Q01.4'),
q(4,'A 3.0 kg trolley moving right at 4.0 m/s sticks to a 1.0 kg trolley moving left at 2.0 m/s. Calculate their shared final velocity, assuming no resultant external force.','- Choose right positive: initial momentum = 3.0 × 4.0 + 1.0 × (−2.0) = **10 kg m/s**.\n- Total mass = **4.0 kg**.\n- Conserved momentum gives 4.0v = 10.\n- **v = 2.5 m/s right**.','4.5.7.2','2023 2H Q05: momentum context'),
q(4,'A 0.50 kg ball moving right at 6.0 m/s rebounds left at 4.0 m/s in 0.10 s. Find the average force on the ball.','- Right positive: u = +6.0, v = −4.0 m/s.\n- Δp = m(v − u) = 0.50 × (−4.0 − 6.0) = **−5.0 kg m/s**.\n- F = Δp/Δt = −5.0/0.10 = **−50 N**.\n- The force is **50 N left**.','4.5.7.3'),
q(4,'Use the graph to calculate acceleration during the first 4 s and total displacement in 7 s.\n\n![Velocity increases from zero to 8 m/s in four seconds, then remains at 8 m/s for three seconds.](../Diagrams/velocity-time-question.svg)','- Acceleration = gradient = (8 − 0)/(4 − 0) = **2 m/s²**.\n- Triangle area = ½ × 4 × 8 = **16 m**.\n- Rectangle area = 3 × 8 = **24 m**.\n- Total displacement = **40 m**.','4.5.6.1.5'),
q(4,'Explain the effect of doubling a car’s speed on thinking distance and braking distance, assuming fixed reaction time, mass and braking force.','- Thinking distance = vt, so it **doubles**.\n- Kinetic energy = ½mv², so it **quadruples**.\n- Braking work Fs removes that energy; with fixed F, braking distance **quadruples**.\n- Total stopping distance is the sum, so its increase depends on the original two distances; it does not simply double.','4.5.6.3','2022 2HR Q03; 2023 2H Q03'),
q(4,'A piston experiences 120 N over 6.0 cm². Calculate the pressure in pascals.','- 1 cm² = **10⁻⁴ m²**.\n- Area = **0.00060 m²**.\n- p = F/A = 120/0.00060.\n- **200,000 Pa**.','4.5.5.1.1','2023 2H Q01.6–01.7'),
q(6,'Plan an investigation of force and spring extension. Include how to find the spring constant and test whether deformation is elastic.','- Secure a spring to a stable stand with a ruler and pointer beside it. Measure the **unloaded length**.\n- Add known masses in steps, including the hanger in total mass. Calculate force using **F = mg**.\n- Let the spring settle, read length at eye level and calculate **extension = loaded − unloaded length**.\n- Repeat readings and plot force vertically against extension horizontally, using metres. The straight section’s **gradient is k**.\n- Remove loads and check return to the original length to test **elastic recovery**. Loss of proportionality and permanent deformation are not necessarily the same point.\n- Keep the spring/setup fixed, avoid overloading, secure the stand and protect against falling masses or a snapping spring.','4.5.3'),
q(6,'Describe how to investigate the effect of resultant driving force on the acceleration of a trolley system while keeping total moving mass constant.','- Use a low-friction trolley connected by string over a pulley to a mass hanger. Secure the track and pulley.\n- Use light gates or a motion sensor to measure velocity changes and calculate **a = Δv/Δt** before the hanger hits the floor.\n- Move masses from the trolley to the hanger: **total moving mass remains constant**, while hanging weight increases.\n- For the whole moving system, driving force is approximately **hanger mass × g**, allowing for friction. It is not simply the tension force on the trolley alone.\n- Repeat for several forces, calculate mean accelerations and plot **a against F**. An approximately straight line through the origin supports direct proportionality.\n- Control friction and starting conditions, keep the string taut and catch the trolley safely.','4.5.6.2.2'),
q(6,'A 1,000 kg car travels at 20 m/s. Reaction time is 0.70 s and braking force is constant at 5,000 N. Calculate stopping distance and explain how wet road conditions could change it.','- Thinking distance = 20 × 0.70 = **14 m**.\n- Kinetic energy = ½ × 1,000 × 20² = **200,000 J**.\n- Braking work equals energy removed, so braking distance = 200,000/5,000 = **40 m**.\n- Total stopping distance = 14 + 40 = **54 m**.\n- A wet road can reduce tyre grip and the effective braking force, increasing the distance needed to remove the same kinetic energy.\n- If speed and reaction time are unchanged, thinking distance stays 14 m; the increase is in the braking part.','4.5.6.3','2022 2HR Q03; 2023 2H Q03')]
write_bank(5,items,'Motion calculations and braking reasoning occur in both sampled papers (2022 replacement Paper 2HR Q01 and Q03; 2023 Paper 2H Q03 and Q05). Force-balance reasoning is illustrated by 2022 Q04 and 2023 Q02. Pressure examples occur in 2023 Q01 and momentum/safety in Q05. These overlapping broad types are counted once per paper. Additional practice covers spring and acceleration practicals, moments, vectors, fluid pressure and collision calculations.')
